// ─── OOP Interview Questions (200) ──────────────────────────────────────
export const OOPS_QUESTIONS = [
  // ── Easy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)?", "easy", "OOP is a programming paradigm based on 'objects' containing data (fields) and behavior (methods). Contrasts with procedural programming (functions + data separately). OOP organizes code into reusable, self-contained units. Benefits: modularity, reusability, maintainability, easier debugging, real-world modeling. Languages: Java, C++, Python, C#, JavaScript (prototype-based), TypeScript, Ruby, Swift, Kotlin."],
  ["What are the four pillars of OOP?", "easy", "1) Encapsulation: bundling data + methods, hiding internal state via access modifiers. 2) Inheritance: child class inherits properties/methods from parent (IS-A relationship). 3) Polymorphism: same interface, different implementations (compile-time overloading, runtime overriding). 4) Abstraction: hiding implementation details, exposing only essential features (abstract classes, interfaces). Together they enable modular, extensible, and maintainable code."],
  ["What is a class in OOP?", "easy", "A class is a BLUEPRINT for creating objects. Defines: fields (state/attributes), methods (behavior/operations), constructors (initialization), access modifiers (visibility). Classes are compile-time abstractions — they define the structure. Example: class Car { String color; void drive() { ... } }. Not instantiated directly — objects are instances of classes."],
  ["What is an object in OOP?", "easy", "An object is an INSTANCE of a class — runtime entity with state (field values) and behavior (method access). Memory allocated on heap. Each object has its own copy of instance fields. Example: Car myCar = new Car(); myCar.color = 'red'; myCar.drive(). Objects are the runtime manifestation of classes — multiple objects can be created from one class."],
  ["What is encapsulation? Give an example.", "easy", "Encapsulation: bundling data (fields) and methods that operate on that data, restricting direct access to internal state. Achieved via: private fields, public getters/setters. Benefits: data hiding (prevent invalid states), loose coupling (internal changes don't affect callers), maintainability. Example: class BankAccount { private double balance; public double getBalance() { return balance; } public void deposit(double amt) { if (amt > 0) balance += amt; } }."],
  ["What is inheritance? Give an example.", "easy", "Inheritance: child class (subclass) acquires properties and methods of parent class (superclass). Use 'extends' keyword (Java) or ':' (C++/C#). Supports IS-A relationship. Single inheritance: one parent. Multiple inheritance: multiple parents (C++, not Java). Example: class Animal { void eat() { ... } } class Dog extends Animal { void bark() { ... } } — Dog inherits eat(), adds bark(). Enables code reuse and polymorphism."],
  ["What is polymorphism? Give an example.", "easy", "Polymorphism: same method name behaves differently based on object type. Compile-time (overloading): same method name, different parameters. Runtime (overriding): child provides specific implementation for parent method. Example: Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak(); // calls Dog's speak(), not Animal's. Without polymorphism: if/else chains checking object type. With: single method call dispatched correctly based on actual type."],
  ["What is abstraction? Give an example.", "easy", "Abstraction: hiding implementation complexity, exposing only essential features. Achieved via: abstract classes (partial abstraction) and interfaces (full abstraction). User interacts with high-level contract, not implementation details. Example: abstract class Database { abstract void connect(); abstract void query(); } class MySQL extends Database { void connect() { /* MySQL-specific */ } } — caller uses Database without knowing MySQL internals."],
  ["What is a constructor?", "easy", "Constructor: special method that initializes object when created via 'new'. Same name as class, no return type. Types: default (no args), parameterized, copy constructor (creates copy). Called once per object — before first use. If no constructor defined, compiler provides default no-arg constructor. Example: class Person { Person(String name) { this.name = name; } }."],
  ["What is the difference between a class and an object?", "easy", "Class: blueprint/template, compile-time entity, defines structure, stored in code area. Object: instance of class, runtime entity, has state (field values), stored in heap. Multiple objects from one class. Class = cookie cutter; Object = actual cookie. Car class defines properties and methods; myCar object has specific values (color=red, speed=0)."],
  ["What are instance variables vs class variables?", "easy", "Instance variables: unique per object, defined without 'static', belong to instance, accessed via object reference. Each object has own copy. Class variables (static): shared across all instances, defined with 'static', belong to class, accessed via class name. One copy for all objects. Example: class Employee { String name; // instance static int companyTaxId; // class }."],
  ["What are instance methods vs static methods?", "easy", "Instance methods: operate on instance data, need object to call, can access instance/static variables. Static methods: belong to class, don't need object, cannot access instance variables (no 'this'), called via class name. Example: Math.sqrt() (static) vs myList.size() (instance). Static methods for utility operations; instance methods for object behavior."],
  ["What is the 'this' keyword in a class?", "easy", "'this' refers to the current object instance. Uses: distinguish instance fields from parameters (this.name = name), call another constructor (this() constructor chaining), pass current object as parameter (method(this)). Inside instance methods: 'this' is implicitly available. Static methods have no 'this' (no instance context)."],
  ["What is method overriding?", "easy", "Method overriding: child class provides its own implementation of a method defined in parent. Rules: same method signature (name + parameters), same or covariant return type, access modifier at least as accessible (protected → public allowed, public → private not), not private/final/static methods. @Override annotation (optional) helps catch errors. Enables runtime polymorphism — JVM determines which method to call based on object type."],
  ["What is method overloading?", "easy", "Method overloading: multiple methods with same name but DIFFERENT parameters (number, type, or order). Compile-time polymorphism — resolved during compilation. Example: print(int) and print(String) and print(int, String). Return type alone cannot differentiate (compiler error). Constructors can be overloaded. Static methods can be overloaded. Common: constructors, utility methods (Math.min(int, int), Math.min(double, double))."],
  ["What is an interface in OOP?", "easy", "Interface: contract defining WHAT a class must do, not HOW. Contains abstract methods (no body) and default/static methods (Java 8+). All methods implicitly public abstract. Fields are public static final (constants). Class 'implements' interface, must provide all abstract methods. Supports multiple inheritance (class implements multiple interfaces). Example: interface Drawable { void draw(); } class Circle implements Drawable { void draw() { ... } }. Enables polymorphism and loose coupling."],
  ["What is an abstract class?", "easy", "Abstract class: class that cannot be instantiated (cannot create objects directly). May have abstract methods (no body, must be overridden) and concrete methods (with implementation). Declared with 'abstract' keyword. Subclass 'extends' abstract class, must implement all abstract methods (or be abstract itself). Partial abstraction — can have fields, constructors, implemented methods. Example: abstract class Shape { abstract double area(); }."],
  ["What is the difference between public, private, and protected access modifiers?", "easy", "public: accessible from ANY class (everyone). private: accessible only within the SAME class (no external access). protected: accessible within same package + subclasses (even in different packages). Default (package-private, no modifier): accessible within same package only. Encapsulation rule: fields usually private, methods public/protected as needed."],
  ["What is a getter and setter?", "easy", "Getters/setters provide controlled access to private fields. Getter: returns field value (getFieldName()). Setter: sets field value with validation (setFieldName(value)). Enables encapsulation — internal state cannot be directly modified. Example: private int age; public int getAge() { return age; } public void setAge(int age) { if (age >= 0) this.age = age; }. Lombok can auto-generate getters/setters."],
  ["What is a destructor / finalizer?", "easy", "Destructor: method called when object is destroyed. In C++: ~ClassName(), deterministic cleanup (scope exit). In Java: finalize() (deprecated, unreliable). C#/Python: ~ClassName() / __del__ — non-deterministic (GC). Modern practice: use try-with-resources (AutoCloseable), Dispose pattern, or using statement. Deterministic cleanup > relying on finalizers. Garbage-collected languages: destructors are rarely needed (GC handles memory)."],
  ["What is single inheritance?", "easy", "Single inheritance: a class inherits from ONLY ONE parent class. Java, C#, TypeScript support only single inheritance for classes (extends one class). Avoids diamond problem. Multiple inheritance achieved via interfaces (multiple interfaces can be implemented). Example: class Dog extends Animal { } — Dog has ONE parent (Animal)."],
  ["What is multiple inheritance and what problem can it cause?", "easy", "Multiple inheritance: class inherits from MORE THAN ONE parent class. Supported in C++ (class D: public B, public C). Problem: Diamond Problem — if B and C override same method from A, which version does D inherit? Java/C# avoid this by allowing multiple interface inheritance only. C++ resolves via virtual inheritance. Diamond problem: ambiguity in method resolution."],
  ["What is a superclass and a subclass?", "easy", "Superclass (parent, base): class being inherited from. Subclass (child, derived): class that inherits. Subclass IS-A superclass. Subclass extends superclass with additional fields/methods. Subclass objects can be used wherever superclass objects are expected (polymorphism). Example: Animal (superclass) → Dog (subclass). Dog IS-A Animal."],
  ["What does the super keyword do?", "easy", "super refers to the parent class. Uses: call parent constructor (super() — must be first line in child constructor), access parent fields/methods (super.fieldName, super.methodName()). Constructor chaining: super() calls parent's no-arg constructor automatically if not explicit. Example: class Dog extends Animal { Dog() { super('canine'); // calls Animal(String) constructor } }."],
  ["What is composition vs inheritance?", "easy", "Composition: HAS-A relationship — object contains another object. Inheritance: IS-A relationship — child extends parent. Prefer composition over inheritance: more flexible (can change behavior at runtime via different composed objects), less coupling, no fragile base class problem. Example: Car HAS-A Engine (composition) vs Car IS-A Vehicle (inheritance). Composition enables code reuse without inheritance's limitations."],
  ["What is a static method and when would you use one?", "easy", "Static method: belongs to class (not instance), called via ClassName.method(). Cannot access instance fields (no 'this'). Use for: utility/helper functions (Math.max, Collections.sort), factory methods (Integer.valueOf), main entry point, singleton accessor (getInstance). Static methods don't need object state — they operate on parameters."],
  ["What is a singleton? How do you implement one?", "easy", "Singleton: ensures only ONE instance of a class exists globally. Simple implementation: private constructor, static getInstance() method returning single instance. Thread-safe: eager initialization (static final instance created at class load) or double-checked locking with volatile. Example: class Logger { private static final Logger INSTANCE = new Logger(); private Logger() {} public static Logger getInstance() { return INSTANCE; } }. Use with caution — global state can make testing difficult."],
  ["What is an instance of a class?", "easy", "An instance is a concrete OBJECT created from a class blueprint. Created via 'new' keyword (or reflection, cloning, deserialization). Each instance has: unique memory address, own copy of instance fields, access to all instance methods. Example: new Car() creates an instance of Car. The term 'instance of a class' = 'object of that class'."],
  ["What is method chaining?", "easy", "Method chaining: each method returns the object itself (return this), allowing consecutive calls. Example: builder.setName('John').setAge(30).setCity('NY').build(). Common in: Builder pattern, fluent interfaces, StringBuilder, jQuery. Each method modifies the object and returns it for next call. Requires non-void return type (the object type)."],
  ["What is the difference between pass by value and pass by reference?", "easy", "Pass by value: copy of the value is passed — changes inside method don't affect original. Pass by reference: reference/pointer is passed — changes affect original. Java is ALWAYS pass by value: primitives pass the value, objects pass the reference value (copy of reference). The reference copy still points to same object — modifying object fields affects original, but reassigning the reference doesn't. C#: ref/out for pass by reference. JavaScript: same as Java (pass by value of reference)."],

  // ── Medium ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface?", "med", "Abstract class: can have fields, constructors, concrete methods, abstract methods. Single inheritance (extends). IS-A relationship. Can have access modifiers other than public. Interface: only abstract methods (Java 8+: default/static), constants (public static final). Multiple inheritance (implements). CAN-DO capability. Java 8+: interfaces can have default methods with implementation. Modern guidance: use interfaces for behavior contracts; abstract classes for shared state/complex hierarchy."],
  ["What is the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)?", "med", "LSP: objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass WITHOUT altering program correctness. Subclass should: accept same preconditions, satisfy same postconditions, preserve invariants. Violation: Square extending Rectangle (setWidth changes height? breaks Rectangle expectations). Fix: make Rectangle and Square immutable, or use composition. LSP is the 'L' in SOLID — ensures inheritance doesn't break behavior."],
  ["What is the Open/Closed Principle?", "med", "OCP: classes should be OPEN for extension but CLOSED for modification. Add new functionality by extending (new subclass, new strategy) — NOT by modifying existing, tested code. Example: payment processor — add new payment type by implementing PaymentMethod interface, not by adding if/else to existing processor. Achieved via: interfaces, abstract classes, strategy pattern. OCP is the 'O' in SOLID."],
  ["What is the Single Responsibility Principle?", "med", "SRP: a class should have ONLY ONE reason to change (one responsibility). Each class handles one part of functionality. Violation: a Report class that both generates report data AND formats HTML AND saves to file. Fix: separate classes for ReportGenerator, ReportFormatter, ReportRepository. Easier: if you can describe a class's responsibility using 'and' — it likely violates SRP. SRP is the 'S' in SOLID."],
  ["What is the Interface Segregation Principle?", "med", "ISP: clients should NOT be forced to depend on interfaces they don't use. Large interfaces should be split into smaller, specific ones. Violation: Worker interface with work(), eat(), sleep() — Robot class forced to implement eat() and sleep() (cannot). Fix: separate Workable, Eatable, Sleepable interfaces. Class implements only what it needs. ISP is the 'I' in SOLID."],
  ["What is the Dependency Inversion Principle?", "med", "DIP: high-level modules should NOT depend on low-level modules; BOTH should depend on ABSTRACTIONS. Abstractions should NOT depend on details; details depend on abstractions. Example: BusinessLogic directly depends on MySQLDatabase — changing database requires changing BusinessLogic. Fix: BusinessLogic depends on Database interface; MySQL and PostgreSQL implement the interface. DI containers (Spring, Guice) automate wiring. DIP is the 'D' in SOLID."],
  ["What are SOLID principles? Name all five.", "med", "S — Single Responsibility: one reason to change. O — Open/Closed: open for extension, closed for modification. L — Liskov Substitution: subtypes replaceable for supertypes. I — Interface Segregation: small, focused interfaces. D — Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions, not concretions. SOLID enables maintainable, testable, extensible OOP code."],
  ["What is duck typing?", "med", "Duck typing: 'If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck.' Object's suitability is determined by PRESENCE OF METHODS/PROPERTIES, not its type. Used in: Python, JavaScript, Ruby. No explicit interface needed — just implement the expected methods. Example: if obj.quack() works, treat it as a duck. Static typing (Java, C#): compile-time type checks. Dynamic typing + duck typing: more flexible but less safe."],
  ["What is the difference between early binding and late binding?", "med", "Early binding (compile-time): method to call is determined at compile time. Static methods, private methods, final methods — resolved based on REFERENCE type. Faster (no runtime lookup). Late binding (runtime/dynamic dispatch): method determined at runtime based on ACTUAL OBJECT type. Overridden virtual methods — uses vtable. Slight overhead. Example: Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak() — late binding calls Dog.speak()."],
  ["What is dynamic dispatch / runtime polymorphism?", "med", "Dynamic dispatch: JVM/runtime determines which overridden method to call based on ACTUAL object type (not reference type). Uses virtual method table (vtable): each class has table of method pointers. When overridden method called: lookup in object's actual class vtable → execute correct implementation. Enables polymorphism: parent reference can invoke child's implementation. Not used for: static, private, final methods (compile-time binding)."],
  ["What is the difference between shallow copy and deep copy?", "med", "Shallow copy: copies object's fields AS-IS — references share same underlying objects (changes in copy reflect in original). Cloneable interface default (shallow). Deep copy: recursively copies all referenced objects — completely independent copy. Changes to copy don't affect original. Methods: serialization/deserialization, copy constructors, manual cloning of all referenced objects. Shallow = fast but shared; Deep = slow but independent."],
  ["What is method hiding vs method overriding?", "med", "Method hiding: static method in child with same signature as parent static method. The method is HIDDEN, not overridden — which method runs depends on REFERENCE type (compile-time). Method overriding: instance method in child overrides parent's instance method — which runs depends on OBJECT type (runtime). Key difference: hiding = static/compile-time; overriding = instance/runtime. @Override annotation only works for instance methods."],
  ["What is covariant return type?", "med", "Covariant return type: overriding method can return a MORE SPECIFIC type than the parent method. Example: class Animal { Animal clone() { ... } } class Dog extends Animal { @Override Dog clone() { ... } } — returns Dog instead of Animal. Reduces need for casting. Supported in Java 5+. Not supported for primitives or void."],
  ["What is an association, aggregation, and composition in UML/OOP?", "med", "Association: general relationship between classes (uses-a). Aggregation: HAS-A with INDEPENDENT lifetimes — parts can exist without whole. Example: Car has Wheel — wheels can exist without car (can be on shelf). Composition: HAS-A with DEPENDENT lifetimes — parts cannot exist without whole. Example: House has Room — rooms created/destroyed with house. Composition uses 'owned by' semantics; aggregation is weaker."],
  ["What is the difference between IS-A and HAS-A relationships?", "med", "IS-A: inheritance — subclass IS-A superclass (Dog IS-A Animal). Represents type/subtype. Implemented via extends/implements. HAS-A: composition/aggregation — class HAS-A member of another type (Car HAS-A Engine). Represents containment. Prefer HAS-A over IS-A: more flexible, less coupling. Example: Car IS-A Vehicle (inheritance), Car HAS-A Engine (composition). Both can coexist."],
  ["What is the Factory design pattern?", "med", "Factory: creates objects WITHOUT exposing creation logic to caller. Client calls factory method with parameters; factory returns appropriate object type. Encapsulates object creation (Single Responsibility). Example: ShapeFactory.createShape('circle') returns new Circle(). Variations: Simple Factory, Factory Method (subclass decides which concrete class to instantiate), Abstract Factory (family of related objects). Reduces coupling between client and concrete classes."],
  ["What is the Observer design pattern?", "med", "Observer: one-to-many dependency — when subject changes state, all observers are notified automatically. Subject: maintains observer list, notify() broadcasts updates. Observers: update() method called on notification. Loose coupling — subject and observers don't know each other's details. Example: blog publisher → email subscribers, stock exchange → trading bots. Used in: event listeners, MVC (Model notifies View), reactive programming."],
  ["What is the Strategy design pattern?", "med", "Strategy: defines family of ALGORITHMS, encapsulates each, makes them interchangeable. Client selects strategy at runtime. Avoids if/else for algorithm selection. Composition over inheritance: strategies are composed, not inherited. Example: PaymentProcessor with PaymentStrategy (CreditCard, PayPal, Crypto). Sorting with SortStrategy (QuickSort, MergeSort). Open/Closed Principle: new strategies added without modifying existing code."],
  ["What is the Decorator design pattern?", "med", "Decorator: dynamically adds responsibility to an object WITHOUT modifying its class. Wraps original object with decorator class having same interface. Multiple decorators can be stacked. Example: Coffee (cost $2) + MilkDecorator (+$0.5) + SugarDecorator (+$0.25) = total $2.75. Java I/O: new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream('file.txt')). More flexible than subclassing (combinations via composition). Single Responsibility: each decorator does one thing."],
  ["What is the Adapter design pattern?", "med", "Adapter: converts one interface to another that client expects. Lets incompatible classes work together. Object adapter (composition): adapter wraps adaptee, delegates calls. Class adapter (inheritance): adapter extends both target and adaptee (requires multiple inheritance). Example: USB-C to USB-A adapter. New code expects Logger interface; old library returns ConsoleLogger — Adapter makes Logger wrap ConsoleLogger. Used for: legacy system integration, library wrapping."],
  ["What is the difference between creational, structural, and behavioral patterns?", "med", "Creational: object creation mechanisms (Singleton, Factory, Builder, Prototype, Abstract Factory). Structural: class/object composition (Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, Facade, Composite, Flyweight, Bridge). Behavioral: communication between objects (Observer, Strategy, Command, Template Method, Iterator, State, Visitor, Mediator, Chain of Responsibility, Memento, Interpreter). Creational → how objects are created. Structural → how objects are composed. Behavioral → how objects interact."],
  ["What is cohesion and coupling? Why do we want high cohesion and low coupling?", "med", "Cohesion: how focused a class's responsibilities are. High cohesion = related methods/fields grouped together. Low cohesion = unrelated responsibilities (God class). Coupling: how dependent one class is on another. Low coupling = changes in one class minimally affect others. Goal: HIGH cohesion, LOW coupling. High cohesion → good Single Responsibility. Low coupling → easy testing, maintenance, and reuse."],
  ["What is the Law of Demeter?", "med", "Law of Demeter (principle of least knowledge): a class should only talk to its immediate friends. Avoid chained calls: a.getB().getC().doSomething(). Instead: a.doSomething() delegates internally. Violation: customer.getWallet().getMoney() — asks about Wallet's internals. Better: customer.pay(amount) — customer knows how to pay. Reduces coupling — changes to Wallet don't break CustomerClient."],
  ["What is the difference between composition and aggregation?", "med", "Composition: STRONG ownership — parts cannot exist without whole. Whole manages part's lifecycle. If whole is destroyed, parts are destroyed. Example: House and Room. Aggregation: WEAK ownership — parts can exist independently. Whole references parts but doesn't manage their lifecycle. Example: Car and Wheel (wheels can exist separately). In UML: composition = filled diamond, aggregation = hollow diamond. Implementation: both use references; difference is lifecycle semantics."],
  ["What is an immutable object and why is it useful?", "med", "Immutable object: state CANNOT change after creation. All fields final, no setters, defensive copies in getters (for mutable fields), constructor fully initializes state. Examples: String, Integer, LocalDate. Benefits: thread-safe (no synchronization needed), cacheable, shareable, no unexpected state changes, safe as hash map keys. Disadvantage: new object for every change (can cause garbage collection pressure). Create via Builder pattern for complex immutables."],
  ["What is the difference between an abstract class and a mixin?", "med", "Abstract class: can have fields, constructors, concrete methods. Single inheritance. Used for IS-A hierarchy. Mixin: reusable code module that can be added to any class. No IS-A relationship — just adds behavior. In languages: Dart mixins, Ruby modules, C++ via multiple inheritance, Java via default methods on interfaces. Mixin provides specific functionality without defining type hierarchy. Prefer mixins when: reusing behavior across unrelated classes."],
  ["What is the template method design pattern?", "med", "Template Method: defines SKELETON of algorithm in parent class, lets subclasses override specific steps. Inherited method calls abstract/primitives that subclasses implement. Controls the WHAT and WHEN; subclasses decide the HOW. Example: DataExporter: readData(), processData(), formatData(), saveData(). Each subclass (PDFExporter, CSVExporter) only implements the parts that differ. Inverse of Strategy: Template Method uses inheritance; Strategy uses composition."],
  ["What is a fluent interface / builder pattern?", "med", "Builder pattern: separates object construction from its representation. Fluent interface: method chaining for readable DSL. Example: User user = new User.Builder().name('John').email('john@x.com').age(30).build(). Benefits: readable, immutable objects (builder sets all fields, build() creates immutable object), handles optional parameters cleanly. Alternatives to telescoping constructors. Essential for objects with many optional fields."],
  ["What is the Command design pattern?", "med", "Command: encapsulates a request as an OBJECT — parameterizes clients with different requests, queues/logs requests, supports undo. Components: Command interface (execute(), undo()), ConcreteCommand invokes receiver action, Invoker calls execute(), Receiver performs actual work. Example: text editor — CopyCommand, PasteCommand, UndoCommand. Each command can be stored in history for undo/redo. Enables: macro recording, job queues, transactional behavior."],
  ["What is the Proxy design pattern?", "med", "Proxy: provides a SURROGATE/placeholder for another object to control access. Types: Virtual proxy (lazy loading — defer expensive object creation), Protection proxy (access control — check permissions), Remote proxy (local representative for remote object — RPC/gRPC), Caching proxy (return cached result). Example: ImageProxy loading real high-resolution Image only when displayed. GOF: same interface as real subject, holds reference."],
  ["What is an enum and how does it relate to OOP?", "med", "Enum: fixed set of named constants (Days of week, Colors). In Java/C#, enums are full-fledged CLASSES — can have fields, methods, constructors. Each enum constant is a SINGLETON instance of the enum type. Example: enum Status { PENDING, APPROVED, REJECTED; boolean isActive() { return this == APPROVED; } }. Enums in OOP: type-safe constants, can implement interfaces, used with switch, singleton pattern for a fixed set."],
  ["What is type casting and what are its risks?", "med", "Type casting: converting object from one type to another. Upcasting: child → parent (safe, implicit). Downcasting: parent → child (risky, explicit). Risks: ClassCastException at runtime if actual type doesn't match. instanceof check before downcasting. Example: Animal a = new Dog(); // upcast safe. Dog d = (Dog) a; // downcast safe (a IS Dog). Cat c = (Cat) a; // ClassCastException! Use instanceof: if (a instanceof Dog) { Dog d = (Dog) a; }."],
  ["What is the difference between overriding toString() and equals()?", "med", "toString(): returns string representation of object. Default: ClassName@hashCode. Override to provide meaningful description. equals(): compares object equality. Default: == (reference equality). Override to define VALUE equality (fields comparison). If equals() overridden, MUST override hashCode() — equal objects must have equal hash codes. Example: Person with id — two Person objects with same id should be equals and have same hashCode."],
  ["What is the difference between instanceof and typeof in OOP context?", "med", "instanceof (Java/JS): checks if object is instance of a class/interface (type hierarchy). true for any class in the inheritance chain. Used before downcasting. typeof (JS/TS): returns type as STRING ('string', 'number', 'object', 'function', 'undefined', 'boolean', 'symbol'). Doesn't check inheritance — only primitive/object type. In OOP: instanceof = 'is this a Dog or subclass?'. typeof = 'what is the JavaScript type of this value?'."],
  ["What is multiple interface implementation?", "med", "Multiple interface implementation: a class can implement MULTIPLE interfaces (Java, C#). class Printer implements Scannable, Printable, Faxable { ... }. Class provides implementation for ALL methods from all interfaces. Avoids diamond problem of multiple inheritance (interfaces don't have state). Enables combining contracts (device IS Scannable AND Printable). Java 8+ default methods in interfaces add complexity (must resolve conflicts explicitly)."],

  // ── Hard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ["What is the diamond problem in multiple inheritance and how is it solved?", "hard", "Diamond problem: class D inherits from B and C, both inherit from A. If B and C override A's method differently, which version does D inherit? C++ resolves via virtual inheritance (class B: virtual public A) — D must explicitly override ambiguous method. Java avoids entirely (no multiple class inheritance). Java 8+ interfaces: if B and C interfaces have default methods with same signature, D MUST override. The diamond shape: A at top, B+C in middle, D at bottom."],
  ["What is virtual dispatch and how does a vtable work?", "hard", "Virtual dispatch: runtime determination of which method to call for overridden methods. Implemented via vtable (virtual method table): each class (not each object) has a table of function pointers. Each object has a vtable pointer (vptr) set by constructor. Calling overridden method: 1) Load vptr from object, 2) Index into vtable, 3) Call function pointer. Cost: one extra indirection. Non-virtual methods: direct call (compile-time). C++: virtual keyword enables vtable; Java: all instance methods are virtual (except private/final/static)."],
  ["What is the difference between nominal typing and structural typing?", "hard", "Nominal typing: type compatibility based on explicit NAME/DECLARATION (class name, interface name). Java, C#, C++: Dog implements Animal → Dog IS-A Animal because it's explicitly declared. Structural typing: type compatibility based on STRUCTURE (shape) — if it has same methods/properties, it's compatible. TypeScript, Go: interface Dog { bark(): void } — any object with bark() method satisfies Dog, even without explicit declaration. More flexible (duck typing at compile-time)."],
  ["What is covariance and contravariance in generics?", "hard", "Covariance: preserve ordering (List<Dog> IS-A List<Animal>?). Java: List<Dog> NOT subtype of List<Animal> (invariant). Use List<? extends Animal> for covariant (read-only). Contravariance: reverse ordering (List<Animal> IS-A List<Dog>?). Use List<? super Dog> for contravariant (write-only). C#: IEnumerable<out T> (covariant), Action<in T> (contravariant). PECS rule: Producer Extends, Consumer Super. Covariance for reading; contravariance for writing."],
  ["What is the Composite design pattern and when would you use it?", "hard", "Composite: treats individual objects and compositions UNIFORMLY (tree structure). Component interface with common operations. Leaf: individual object. Composite: container with children (can include other composites). Example: FileSystem — File (leaf) and Directory (composite, contains files/directories). Both implement FileSystemNode with getSize(). Directory.getSize() sums children. Used for: UI component trees, organizational hierarchies, XML document parsing."],
  ["What is the Flyweight design pattern?", "hard", "Flyweight: minimizes memory usage by SHARING common object state (intrinsic) across many objects. Intrinsic: shared, immutable (character glyph, font). Extrinsic: context-specific (position, color) — computed/stored externally. Example: text editor — each character char+font shared (flyweight), positions stored externally. Without flyweight: 100K characters → 100K objects. With: 100K positions + ~100 font objects. Used in: game terrain rendering (shared texture tile, varied position)."],
  ["What is the Chain of Responsibility pattern?", "hard", "Chain of Responsibility: passes request along a chain of handlers until one handles it. Each handler decides to handle or pass to next. Loose coupling — sender doesn't know which handler processes it. Example: Logger — DEBUG → INFO → ERROR. Log level INFO: passes DEBUG, handles INFO, stops before ERROR. Middleware pipeline (Express.js, ASP.NET Core): request goes through auth→logging→validation→handler. Each middleware can handle or call next()."],
  ["What is the Mediator pattern and how does it reduce coupling?", "hard", "Mediator: defines an object that encapsulates HOW objects interact. Objects communicate via mediator instead of directly. Reduces many-to-many connections to one-to-many (all objects → mediator). Example: ChatRoom (mediator) — users send messages to chat room, not to each other. Air traffic control (mediator) — planes communicate via control tower, not directly. GUI: dialog component mediates between form elements (text field enables button)."],
  ["What is the State design pattern?", "hard", "State: allows object to change its behavior when its internal STATE changes. Object appears to change its class. Context delegates state-specific behavior to current State object. States are interchangeable via same interface. Example: VendingMachine — states: NoCoin, HasCoin, Dispensing. NoCoin: insert coin → transition to HasCoin. HasCoin: select item → dispense → transition back. Without State: large if/else on state field. With: each state encapsulated in its own class."],
  ["What is the Visitor design pattern?", "hard", "Visitor: separates ALGORITHM from OBJECT STRUCTURE. New operations added without modifying element classes. Elements accept(Visitor) — visitor's visit() called with element's type. Double dispatch: method called depends on both element type AND visitor type. Example: AST nodes (Number, Add, Variable) — visitors: EvalVisitor (compute value), PrintVisitor (print expression), TypeCheckVisitor (verify types). Adding new operation = new visitor class (Open/Closed). Adding new element type = modify all visitors (extension challenge)."],
  ["What is the Memento design pattern?", "hard", "Memento: captures and externalizes object's internal state WITHOUT violating encapsulation — for undo/restore. Originator: creates memento with snapshot of its state. Memento: opaque token storing originator's state (often private inner class). Caretaker: stores and manages mementos (history). Example: text editor — Editor creates Memento (snapshot) on each change. Undo: restores from previous memento. Memento is opaque — caretaker cannot inspect it."],
  ["What is Dependency Injection and how does it relate to DIP?", "hard", "Dependency Injection (DI): passing dependencies from outside (typically via constructor) rather than creating them internally. Dependencies are 'injected'. DIP is the principle; DI is a technique to achieve it. Types: constructor injection (best — immutable, required deps), setter injection (optional deps), interface injection. Without DI: class needs Database → new MySQLDatabase() inside class (tight coupling). With DI: class receives Database interface (any database). DI containers (Spring, Guice, Dagger) automate wiring."],
  ["What is an IoC (Inversion of Control) container?", "hard", "IoC container: framework that manages object creation and dependency wiring. Controls lifecycle (when objects are created/destroyed) and scope (singleton, prototype, request). Configuration: XML, annotations, or Java config. Examples: Spring IoC, Google Guice, Dagger. Container: 1) Reads configuration, 2) Creates bean definitions, 3) Resolves dependencies (DI), 4) Provides beans to application. Avoids manual 'new' and wiring code. Inversion: your code doesn't control flow — framework calls your code."],
  ["What is the difference between DI via constructor, setter, and interface?", "hard", "Constructor injection: dependencies passed via constructor. Best for: required dependencies, immutability (final fields), clear contracts. All dependencies available at object creation. Setter injection: dependencies set via setter methods. Best for: optional dependencies, circular dependencies (constructors can't handle circular), reconfiguration. Interface injection: injector calls interface method to set dependency. Less common. Modern guidance: prefer constructor injection (immutability, guaranteed availability)."],
  ["What is the Repository pattern?", "hard", "Repository: mediates between domain and data mapping layers, acting like an in-memory collection. Provides collection-like interface (find, add, remove) for domain objects. Hides data access details (SQL, NoSQL, in-memory). Example: interface UserRepository { User findById(int id); List<User> findAll(); void save(User user); } class UserRepositoryImpl { /* JPA/Hibernate/SQL */ }. Benefits: separation of concerns, testable (mock repository), domain focus (no SQL in business logic)."],
  ["What is the Service Layer / Facade pattern?", "hard", "Service Layer: defines application boundary with a set of operations (use cases). Coordinates business logic, transactions, and security. Facade: simplified interface to complex subsystem. Example: OrderService — placeOrder(), cancelOrder(), getOrderHistory(). Coordinates: call UserService, InventoryService, PaymentService, EmailService. Service layer is a Facade for business operations. Provides: transaction demarcation, security checks, single entry point for each use case. Not business logic — coordinate."],
  ["What is CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)?", "hard", "CQRS: separates read operations (Queries) from write operations (Commands). Different models for reading and writing. Command: changes state (void return). Query: returns data (no side effects). Benefits: independent scaling (read replicas for queries), optimized models (denormalized read model), security (fine-grained permissions). Simple CQRS: separate method categories. Full CQRS: separate databases, event sourcing. Not necessary for simple CRUD apps. Add when read/write patterns differ significantly."],
  ["What is Domain-Driven Design (DDD) and how does it relate to OOP?", "hard", "DDD: software design approach focusing on the DOMAIN (business logic) — not technical concerns. Key concepts: Ubiquitous Language (common terms between devs and domain experts), Bounded Context (explicit boundaries between domain parts), Entities (objects with identity), Value Objects (immutable, defined by attributes), Aggregates (consistency boundaries), Domain Events. DDD uses OOP heavily: encapsulation of business logic in domain objects, rich domain models, inheritance for specialization. Strategic DDD: dividing complex systems into bounded contexts."],
  ["What is an aggregate root in DDD?", "hard", "Aggregate root: the ROOT entity that guarantees consistency of the entire AGGREGATE (cluster of domain objects). External objects can only reference the root, not internal entities. Root controls all access to aggregate. Example: Order (root) with OrderItems. To change OrderItem.quantity: call order.updateItemQuantity(itemId, newQty). Root loads all items, enforces invariants (total > 0), saves all changes atomically. Without root: direct item manipulation could break invariants. Repositories only work with aggregate roots."],
  ["What is the difference between entity and value object in DDD?", "hard", "Entity: has IDENTITY — two entities with same field values but different IDs are DIFFERENT objects. Mutable, changes over time. Example: Customer (identified by customerId). Value Object: defined by ATTRIBUTES only — two VOs with same values are EQUAL. IMMUTABLE. Replaceable. Example: Money(amount, currency), Address(street, city). Money(10, USD) == Money(10, USD) always. Value objects should be immutable; when you change a value, you create a new one. Prefer value objects over primitives ('primitive obsession' anti-pattern)."],
  ["What is metaprogramming and reflection in OOP?", "hard", "Metaprogramming: program that can read, modify, or generate other programs (or itself). Reflection: runtime inspection and modification of classes, methods, fields. Java: Class.forName(), obj.getClass(), method.invoke(), field.set(). Used for: DI containers, ORM frameworks, serialization, testing (mock frameworks), dynamic proxies. Power + risk: can bypass encapsulation (access private fields), slower (runtime resolution), error-prone (ClassNotFoundException). Modern alternatives: annotations, code generation, lambda expressions reduce reflection usage."],
  ["What are mixins and how do they differ from multiple inheritance?", "hard", "Mixins: reusable code module that can be 'mixed into' any class. Adds behavior without IS-A relationship. Multiple inheritance: class inherits from multiple parents — establishes IS-A relationships. Mixins in different languages: Ruby modules, Dart mixins, Scala traits, JavaScript (mixin functions). Java 8+ default methods on interfaces provide mixin-like capabilities. C++: no distinction — multiple inheritance serves both purposes. Mixins avoid the DIAMOND PROBLEM differently: linearization (Ruby, Scala) determines precedence."],
  ["What is the Prototype pattern in OOP?", "hard", "Prototype: creates new objects by CLONING a prototype instance (rather than using constructors). clone() method creates copy. Java: Cloneable interface + super.clone() (shallow copy). Deep copy for composite objects. Useful when: object creation is expensive (database query to build object), many similar objects needed, want to avoid subclassing for different configurations. Example: game — prototype enemy object, clone() each time, customize slightly. Cloneable is a 'marker interface' (no methods)."],
  ["What is the difference between class-based and prototype-based OOP?", "hard", "Class-based (Java, C++, C#, Python): classes are blueprints, objects are instances. Inheritance: class extends class. Clean separation of class vs object. Prototype-based (JavaScript): no classes — objects inherit DIRECTLY from other objects (prototypes). Inheritance: object's __proto__ chains to parent object. Dynamic: can add/change methods on individual objects. JavaScript new: constructs object, sets prototype. ES6 classes are SYNTAXIC SUGAR over prototypes. Prototype-based more dynamic, class-based more structured."],
  ["What is referential transparency and why is it hard to achieve in OOP?", "hard", "Referential transparency: expression can be replaced with its value WITHOUT changing program behavior (pure, no side effects). Functional programming promotes this. OOP challenges: mutable object state (same method call returns different values), side effects (printing, modifying), object identity matters (not just value). Encapsulation helps manage side effects but doesn't eliminate them. Functional core / imperative shell: functional inside (pure logic), OOP outside (manageable side effects)."],
  ["What is the difference between OOP and functional programming? When to choose each?", "hard", "OOP: state + behavior bundled in objects, mutable state, side effects expected, polymorphism via inheritance/interface, design patterns. Functional: pure functions (no side effects), immutable data, first-class functions, declarative, recursion, lambda calculus. Choose OOP for: complex state management, UI frameworks, modeling real-world entities with behavior, team familiarity with OOP. Choose FP for: data transformations, concurrent/parallel processing, mathematical computations, pipelines. Many modern languages multi-paradigm (Kotlin, Scala, JavaScript, Python)."],
  ["What is the God Object anti-pattern?", "hard", "God object: single class that knows TOO MUCH or does TOO MUCH (centralizes all behavior). Violates Single Responsibility Principle. Hard to maintain, test, and extend. Symptoms: class with hundreds of fields/methods, 'Utils' class doing everything, business logic mixed with UI/persistence. Fix: decompose into focused classes (SRP), extract facades for different concerns, use dependency injection. Real-world: 'Manager' classes that do everything (UserManager handles auth, profiles, notifications, billing)."],
  ["What is anemic domain model and why is it considered an anti-pattern?", "hard", "Anemic domain model: domain objects are just DATA BAGS (getters/setters, no behavior). Business logic in separate service classes. Anti-pattern because: OOP is about DATA + BEHAVIOR together. Anemic model is PROCEDURAL in OOP clothing. Example: Anemic: user.setEmail(), emailService.validateEmail(user.getEmail()). Rich: user.changeEmail(newEmail) — validation inside. Benefits of rich domain model: invariants enforced, cohesive logic, no duplicated validation. Not always an anti-pattern — simple CRUD apps may not need rich models."],
  ["What is the difference between polymorphism through inheritance vs through composition?", "hard", "Inheritance polymorphism: subclass overrides parent method. Type determined by class hierarchy. Rigid — class hierarchy fixed at compile time. Composition polymorphism: objects implement same interface/contract. Different implementations can be swapped at runtime. More flexible — Strategy, Command, Decorator patterns rely on composition polymorphism. Example: Inheritance: Animal → Dog, Cat (speak() overridden). Composition: PaymentStrategy interface with CreditCard, PayPal implementations swapped at runtime. Prefer composition polymorphism: more flexible, testable (swap strategy in test)."],
  ["How do you design a parking lot system using OOP principles?", "hard", "Classes: Vehicle (abstract — car, bike, truck), ParkingSpot (size, level, row, available), ParkingLevel (list of spots, capacity), ParkingLot (singleton — levels), Ticket (spot, vehicle, entryTime), PaymentService (calculate fee by duration). Key OOP: Vehicle polymorphism (getSpotSize()), Spot assignment (findAvailableSpot(vehicle)), Encapsulation (ParkingSpot manages availability). Patterns: Singleton (ParkingLot), Factory (createVehicle by type), Strategy (payment calculation). SOLID: each class has single responsibility."],
  ["How do you design a library management system with OOP?", "hard", "Classes: Library (BookRepository, MemberRepository), Book (title, author, ISBN, status), Member (name, borrowed list), Loan (book, member, date), LibraryService (checkout, return, search, reserve). OOP: Book status management (AVAILABLE, BORROWED, RESERVED — State pattern), SearchService (by title/author — Strategy), FeeCalculator for overdue (Strategy). DDD: Loan is an entity (identity = loanId), Book with value objects (ISBN). Repository pattern for data access. SOLID: search, checkin, checkout separate services."],
  ["How do you design an elevator system with OOP?", "hard", "Classes: Elevator (id, currentFloor, direction, state — MOVING/IDLE/DOOR_OPEN, passengers), Floor (number, up/down buttons), Button (type, floor, status), ElevatorController (list of elevators, request queue), Scheduler (algorithm — Strategy: SCAN, FCFS, smart). OOP: Scheduler Strategy pattern (FCFS, Scan, Smart), Elevator State pattern (Idle, Moving, DoorOpen), Observer (Floor buttons notify Controller). Encapsulation: Elevator manages its own movement; Controller assigns requests. Concurrency: thread-safe request handling."],
  ["What is the Tell Don't Ask principle?", "hard", "Tell Don't Ask: objects should be TOLD what to do, not ASKED for data then acted upon. Violation: if (person.getAddress().isValid()) { person.getAddress().sendMail(content); }. Better: person.sendMail(content) — person internally checks address validity. Tell: call a method (tell object to perform). Ask: query state (getter), make decision externally. Tell Don't Ask encourages: behavior-rich objects, encapsulation, DRY (validation logic in one place). Related to: Law of Demeter, Information Expert (GRASP)."],
  ["What is the Null Object pattern and how does it replace null checks?", "hard", "Null Object: provides DEFAULT BEHAVIOR for missing object instead of returning null. Implements same interface, methods do nothing or return safe defaults. Example: interface Logger { void log(String msg); } class ConsoleLogger implements Logger { ... } class NullLogger implements Logger { void log(String msg) { /* do nothing */ } }. Replace: if (logger != null) logger.log(msg) with: logger.log(msg) — NullLogger handles it. Eliminates null checks, NullPointerExceptions. Part of 'Option' type in functional languages (Optional in Java, Option in Scala)."],

  // ── Modern / Industry Conceptual ───────────────────────────────────────────
  ["How do you apply SOLID principles when designing a microservice?", "hard", "S: each microservice has SINGLE business capability (not multiple). O: services extensible via new endpoints/events (not modifying existing). L: service contracts should be substitutable (versions backward compatible). I: design small, focused service interfaces/APIs. D: services depend on abstractions (service interfaces), not concrete implementations. Example: PaymentService depends on PaymentGateway interface (Stripe/PayPal injected). New payment provider = new implementation, no service change."],
  ["When should you favor composition over inheritance in a real-world codebase?", "med", "Favor composition when: 1) Behavior needs to change at runtime (different strategies), 2) Unrelated classes share behavior, 3) Base class is changing frequently (fragile base class problem), 4) Need to combine multiple behaviors (inheritance only gives one chain), 5) Testing: composition easier to mock (swap composed objects), 6) IS-A relationship doesn't truly hold (just sharing code). Use inheritance when: true subtype relationship, behavior largely shared, LSP holds."],
  ["How does dependency injection improve testability of OOP code?", "med", "DI allows injecting MOCK/STUB dependencies in tests. Without DI: class creates its own Database — tests hit real database (slow, stateful). With DI: class receives Database interface — tests inject MockDatabase (fast, controlled responses). Example: test paymentService.processPayment() — inject MockPaymentGateway that returns specific responses (success, failure, timeout). Test each scenario without real payment API. DI enables: isolated unit tests, no external dependencies, reproduce edge cases easily."],
  ["How do you design class hierarchies that are easy to unit test with mocks?", "hard", "Principles: 1) Favor interfaces over concrete classes (dependency injection), 2) Constructor injection (dependencies explicit, cannot be forgotten), 3) Single Responsibility (small classes = focused tests), 4) Avoid deep inheritance (test parent class behavior in isolation), 5) Use factory pattern for complex object creation (mock factory), 6) Prefer pure functions in domain logic (easy to test, no mocks needed). Example: OrderService(InventoryService, PaymentGateway) — both injected, easy to mock separately."],
  ["What is the role of interfaces in achieving loose coupling between modules?", "med", "Interfaces define CONTRACTS without implementation details. Module A depends on Interface I; Module B implements I. Changes to Module B don't affect A (as long as contract maintained). A can't access B's internals. Enables: swapping implementations (test mock → production), independent development (team A: interface consumer; team B: implementer), versioning (stable contract). Example: PaymentService depends on PaymentProcessor interface. Change from Stripe to PayPal = new implementation, no change to PaymentService."],
  ["How would you refactor a God Object into smaller cohesive classes?", "hard", "Steps: 1) Identify distinct responsibilities (GodObject does X, Y, Z), 2) Extract each responsibility into separate class (SRP), 3) Use Facade pattern for backward compatibility (delegates to new classes), 4) Move state (fields) with the behavior, 5) Tests: create tests for extracted classes, then for facade, 6) Incrementally update clients to use new classes instead of facade. Example: UserManager → UserAuth, UserProfile, UserNotification, UserSearch. Facade: UserManager calls these internally, then removed gradually."],
  ["How do value objects help eliminate primitive obsession?", "med", "Primitive obsession: using primitives (String, int) for domain concepts. VO wraps primitives into typed objects. Examples: Email (validates format), Money (currency + amount), PhoneNumber (country code + number + formatting), ZipCode, Temperature. Benefits: type safety (can't pass Email where ZipCode expected), validation (invalid VO can't exist), behavior (email.send(), money.add()), readability (method signatures self-documenting). Prefer VOs over primitives for domain concepts with behavior or validation."],
  ["How do you model domain invariants using encapsulation in DDD?", "hard", "Domain invariants: rules that must ALWAYS be true. Encapsulation enforces invariants by controlling all state changes. Example: Order must have at least one Item. Encapsulation: class Order { private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>(); public void addItem(Item item) { if (item == null) throw ...; items.add(item); } public void removeItem(Item item) { if (items.size() <= 1) throw new DomainException('Order must have at least one item'); items.remove(item); } }. The invariant is enforced — external code cannot break it. Private field + controlled methods = guarded invariant."],
  ["What are the trade-offs of deep inheritance hierarchies in large systems?", "hard", "Cons: fragile base class (change in top class affects all children), deep hierarchy hard to understand/trace, diamond problem risk, over-generalization (wrong abstractions), testing complex (need parent behavior), runtime behavior hard to predict. Pros: code reuse (shared behavior in parent), polymorphism leverage, logical taxonomy. Rule of thumb: max depth 3-4 levels. Prefer composition over inheritance for behavior sharing. Use deep hierarchy only for true IS-A with clear, stable abstractions."],
  ["How does the Hexagonal (Ports and Adapters) architecture relate to OOP?", "hard", "Hexagonal (Ports and Adapters): application core has PORTS (interfaces), external systems have ADAPTERS implementing those ports. Core doesn't know about outside world. OOP relation: Ports = interfaces (abstraction), Adapters = implementations. Core domain = entities + use cases (pure OOP). Dependency injection wires adapters to ports at runtime. Example: Port: OrderRepository interface. Adapters: PostgreSQLOrderRepository, InMemoryOrderRepository (test). Core uses OrderRepository port — completely decoupled from database."],
  ["How do you decide between an abstract class and an interface in modern languages?", "med", "Use abstract class when: shared STATE (fields), common constructor logic, protected methods, close relationship (IS-A), base implementation with extension points. Use interface when: behavior contract, unrelated classes need same capability, multiple inheritance needed, loose decoupling desired. Modern languages blur the line: Java interfaces have default methods, Kotlin has sealed classes/ interfaces. Practical: start with interface (more flexible), use abstract class only when shared state is essential."],
  ["How do default methods on interfaces change interface design?", "med", "Default methods (Java 8+) add implementation right inside interface. Benefits: backward compatibility (add method without breaking existing implementations), provide common behavior across all implementations, template method pattern via interface. Example: interface Sortable { void sort(); default void reverseSort() { Collections.reverse(list()); } }. Cautions: default method conflicts (if two interfaces both have default same-signature method, implementing class MUST override). Default methods blur the line between interface and abstract class."],
  ["How do you avoid tight coupling when using the Singleton pattern?", "hard", "Singleton creates tight coupling: classes directly call Singleton.getInstance(). Fix: 1) Use DI container to manage singleton lifecycle (scoped as singleton, injected via constructor), 2) Implement Singleton interface, inject that interface, 3) Make Singleton testable by allowing replacement. Example: // Bad: Logger.getInstance().log(). // Good: class Service { Logger logger; Service(Logger logger) { ... } }. DI container configures Logger as singleton. Tests inject MockLogger. Decouples: classes don't know about singleton pattern."],
  ["Why is global mutable state through Singletons considered an anti-pattern?", "med", "Problems: hidden dependencies (no declaration of dependency in constructor), testability (impossible to mock/replace in tests), concurrency issues (must be thread-safe), hidden coupling (any class can access and modify), scope issues (different test contexts share state — flaky tests), violates SRP (class manages its own lifecycle + its business). Better: DI container with singleton scope (explicit wiring), or instance passed via constructor (explicit dependency). Singleton is ok for: stateless utility classes (Math), constants, logging frameworks."],
  ["How do immutability and OOP design improve concurrency safety?", "hard", "Immutability: thread-safe by default — no mutable state, no synchronization needed. Objects can be shared freely across threads. OOP: well-encapsulated mutable state with synchronized access (synchronized methods, locks). Combine: immutable value objects for data, mutable entities with encapsulated synchronization. Example: Money (immutable — thread safe), BankAccount (mutable with synchronized debit/credit). Avoid: getters returning references to mutable internal state (defensive copy needed). Immutable objects are simpler to reason about in concurrent code."],
  ["How do you apply the Open/Closed Principle when adding new payment methods?", "med", "Define interface: interface PaymentMethod { PaymentResult process(Order order); }. Existing implementations: CreditCardPayment, PayPalPayment. New method: new class ApplePayPayment implements PaymentMethod. PaymentService uses List<PaymentMethod> — no modification needed. New method = new class. OCP achieved: closed for modification (no if/else in service), open for extension (add new class). If business logic differs significantly: Strategy pattern with selection criteria."],
  ["How would you design an extensible notification system using OOP?", "hard", "Interfaces: NotificationChannel (send(message)). Implementations: EmailChannel, SMSChannel, PushChannel, SlackChannel. Factory/Registry: NotificationChannelRegistry (map of type→channel). Template: NotificationService — orchestrates: get user preferences, loop channels, send via each. Builder: Notification message building. Observer: when event occurs, notify relevant users. Patterns: Strategy (channels), Factory (create by type), Observer (trigger on events). Extensibility: new channel = new class implementing NotificationChannel, register in config."],
  ["What is the difference between a domain model and a data model?", "med", "Domain model: business-logic rich objects with behavior, rules, invariants. May not map 1:1 to database. Example: Order — contains OrderItems as value objects, methods like addItem, calculateTotal. Data model: database representation (tables, columns, foreign keys). May be normalized, optimized for queries. Example: orders table, order_items table with order_id foreign key. ORM (Hibernate, Entity Framework) maps between them. Sometimes identical, often different — especially in complex domains (CQRS separates read/write models fully)."],
  ["How does the Repository pattern decouple business logic from persistence?", "med", "Repository provides COLLECTION-LIKE INTERFACE for domain objects. Business logic uses repository (e.g., repository.findById(id)) without knowing about database. Benefits: business logic doesn't contain SQL/JPA/Hibernate, persistence technology can change (MySQL→MongoDB), testable (mock repository), domain focus. Example: // Business logic uses UserRepository interface — no SQL. repo.findActiveUsers() — could be JPA query, native SQL, or REST call. Underlying implementation invisible to business logic. Clean separation: one layer for business, one for persistence."],
  ["How do you use the Strategy pattern to remove large conditional blocks?", "med", "Replace if/else or switch blocks with Strategy. Before: if (type == 'PDF') { /* PDF export */ } else if (type == 'CSV') { /* CSV export */ } else if (type == 'JSON') { /* JSON export */ }. After: interface ExportStrategy { void export(Data data); } class PDFExport implements ExportStrategy { ... } class CSVExport implements ExportStrategy { ... } class JSONExport implements ExportStrategy { ... } Map<String, ExportStrategy> strategies = Map.of('pdf', new PDFExport(), 'csv', new CSVExport(), 'json', new JSONExport()); strategies.get(type).export(data). Open/Closed: new formats added without modifying existing code."],
  ["How do design patterns help with maintainability in a growing codebase?", "med", "Patterns provide: PROVEN SOLUTIONS to common problems, COMMON VOCABULARY (team says 'Observer' not 'let's make a listener system'), PRINCIPLES embodied (SOLID, composition over inheritance), PREDICTABLE STRUCTURE (new team members understand faster). Anti-pattern avoided: reinventing bad solutions. Patterns are not silver bullets — misapplied patterns cause unnecessary complexity. Use patterns where they solve REAL problems. Start simple — extract patterns when code shows the need (not up front)."],
  ["When is inheritance the wrong tool and what alternatives exist?", "med", "Inheritance wrong when: code reuse is the only goal (use composition/delegation), IS-A relationship doesn't hold ('Horse is a Vehicle' is wrong — Horse is an Animal), behavior needs to change at runtime (use Strategy), class hierarchy is deep/fragile (use composition), LSP would be violated (subclass incompatible with parent expectations). Alternatives: Composition (delegate to helper objects), Strategy pattern (swappable algorithms), Decorator (add behavior dynamically), Interfaces + default methods (share behavior without inheritance)."],
  ["How do you handle cross-cutting concerns like logging without polluting domain classes?", "hard", "Cross-cutting concerns (logging, transaction management, security, auditing) shouldn't be mixed with domain logic. Solutions: 1) Aspect-Oriented Programming (Spring AOP, AspectJ) — intercept method calls via proxies, 2) Decorator pattern — wrap service with LoggingDecorator, 3) Proxy pattern — CGLIB/JDK proxy for automatic logging, 4) Event-driven approach — domain events published, subscribers handle cross-cutting (auditing), 5) Filter/Interceptor (web filters for request logging). Best: AOP or Proxy — zero pollution of domain code."],
  ["How does encapsulation help prevent invalid object states in concurrent code?", "hard", "Encapsulation controls ALL paths that modify internal state. In concurrent code: synchronized methods/locks protect invariants. Private fields + controlled access methods = single point of synchronization. Example: class Counter { private int count; public synchronized void increment() { count++; } public synchronized int getCount() { return count; } }. Without encapsulation: callers could directly manipulate count, missing synchronization. Encapsulation ensures all access goes through thread-safe methods. Rule: if state is mutable and shared — encapsulate it with proper synchronization."],
  ["How would you design a role-based access control system with OOP?", "hard", "Classes: User (id, roles), Role (name, permissions), Permission (resource, action). Check: user.hasPermission('document', 'READ'). Patterns: Decorator — wrap service methods with authorization check: AuthorizedDocumentService(DocumentService) checks permission before delegating. Strategy — different authorization strategies (RBAC, ABAC). Proxy — AuthorizingProxy for each service. Interface: UserContext (get current user from security context). Implementation: Spring Security (filters, annotations, ExpressionBasedSecurity). Core: encapsulated authorization logic — services don't check permissions manually."],
  ["How do you apply the Interface Segregation Principle to a large service interface?", "med", "Large interface: UserService with methods related to auth, profile, notifications, billing, admin. ISP violation: clients that only need auth (login/logout) depend on billing and admin methods. Fix: split into focused interfaces: AuthService, ProfileService, NotificationService, BillingService, AdminService. Each client depends on only what it uses. Implementation class (UserServiceImpl) implements ALL interfaces — but each client sees only its needed slice. Avoid 'fat' interfaces that force clients to implement unused methods."],
  ["What is anemic vs rich domain model and which do you prefer and why?", "hard", "Anemic: objects as data bags (getters/setters only), logic in services. Example: user.setEmail() / emailService.validate(user.getEmail()). Rich: objects with behavior. Example: user.changeEmail(newEmail) — validates internally. Prefer rich domain model for COMPLEX domains (finance, healthcare, logistics) where business logic and rules are significant. Rich model: invariants encapsulated, business logic in one place, follows OOP principles. Rich model may be overengineering for SIMPLE domains (CRUD CRUD apps — anemic + services is fine). Hybrid: rich entities for core domain, anemic DTOs for data transfer."],
  ["How do you design for testability while keeping classes encapsulated?", "hard", "Principles: constructor injection (dependencies explicit), interfaces for external dependencies (mockable), pure logic separated from side effects (functional core), avoid new inside methods (factory injection), avoid static methods (global state, hard to replace). Trade-off: encapsulation sometimes reduced for testability (package-private access for test). Tools: Mockito (mock interfaces), Test containers (real DB in test), in-memory implementations of repositories. Testability doesn't require sacrificing encapsulation: well-designed interfaces + DI = both encapsulated and testable."],
  ["How do generics improve type safety and reuse in OOP languages?", "med", "Generics: parameterize types — List<String> ensures only Strings, without casting. Type safety: compile-time type checks — prevents putting Integer in List<String>. Reuse: one generic class works for any type (List<T>, Map<K,V>, Optional<T>). Avoids code duplication (no separate StringList, IntList). Example: class Box<T> { private T value; public T get() { return value; } public void set(T value) { this.value = value; } }. Box<Item> can only hold Item. Without generics: casting required, runtime ClassCastException risk."],
  ["How would you model a state machine using the State pattern?", "hard", "State pattern: each state as separate class implementing State interface. Context has State field, delegates actions to current state. States transition by setting Context's state. Example: VendingMachine (Context): States — IdleState, HasCoinState, DispensingState. State interface: insertCoin(), selectItem(), dispense(), refund(). IdleState.insertCoin() → context.setState(new HasCoinState()). HasCoinState.selectItem() → if sufficient: context.setState(new DispensingState()). Each state implements just relevant transitions. New state = new class (Open/Closed)."],
  ["How does polymorphism reduce branching logic in a payment processor?", "med", "Without polymorphism: switch (paymentType) { case 'CREDIT_CARD': ... case 'PAYPAL': ... case 'CRYPTO': ... }. Each new type = new case. With polymorphism: PaymentMethod interface (process(order)). Map<String, PaymentMethod> processors. processor.process(order). Each type has its own class. New type = new class implementing PaymentMethod (no branching logic changes). Benefits: Open/Closed, each payment logic isolated, testable independently, no switch duplication across codebase."],
  ["How do you choose between static factory methods and constructors?", "med", "Static factory methods vs constructors: naming (of(value), create(), newInstance() vs same-as-class constructor), caching (factory can return cached instance, constructor always new), subtyping (factory returns any subtype, constructor returns exact type), concise (constructor for simple cases). Use factory method when: complex construction logic, named construction (valueOf, from), returning different subtypes, singleton/instance control, immutability (with caching). Use constructor for: simple creation, no special requirements. Example: Optional.of(), LocalDate.now(), Collections.emptyList() — all factory methods."],
  ["How do you design immutable builder objects for thread safety?", "hard", "Builder: mutable during construction, build() creates immutable object. Thread safety: 1) Don't reuse builder across threads (each thread creates its own), 2) Builder has no shared mutable state, 3) build() creates new object (builder state not shared). Example: class ImmutablePerson { private final String name; private ImmutablePerson(Builder b) { this.name = b.name; } public static class Builder { private String name; public Builder name(String n) { this.name = n; return this; } public ImmutablePerson build() { return new ImmutablePerson(this); } } }. Builder is single-threaded; built object is thread-safe."],
  ["How would you apply the Decorator pattern to add features without subclass explosion?", "med", "Subclass explosion: Coffee → CoffeeWithMilk, CoffeeWithSugar, CoffeeWithMilkAndSugar, CoffeeWithWhippedCream... (each combination needs class). Decorator: Coffee interface (cost()). SimpleCoffee implements Coffee. MilkDecorator wraps Coffee, adds cost. SugarDecorator wraps Coffee. Usage: new MilkDecorator(new SugarDecorator(new SimpleCoffee())). Combinations via stacking decorators — no subclass for each combination. Each decorator adds ONE feature (SRP). Works when: features are optional and combinable."],
  ["How do you balance encapsulation with the need for serialization?", "hard", "Serialization often needs access to private fields (reflection, getters). Solutions: 1) Keep encapsulation — provide serialization framework access via reflection (most Java serializers work on private fields), 2) Use DTOs separate from domain objects (domain = encapsulated, DTO = serializable with public getters), 3) Jackson annotations (@JsonProperty on getters, @JsonIgnore on sensitive), 4) Encapsulated builder + serialization constructor (private constructor for deserialization, builder for client). Trade-off: some exposure for serialization is acceptable — keep domain encapsulation, expose only what's needed for serialization."],
];
